 "reports_individuals_with_tnfaip3_variant": true,
 "relevance_note": "Single-patient case report of a male neonate with arterial tortuosity syndrome: \"Our patient's diagnosis was confirmed with genetic testing.\"",
 "method_note": "Read the whole paper; built the JSON with a short script in scratch, all values from my reading. One in-scope person (the single unnamed patient, label 'patient'). The mother is described (chronic hypertension, pregnancy complications) but is neither tested nor said to be affected, so she gets no row. No SLC2A10 variant is printed: the paper says only that the diagnosis was confirmed with genetic testing, and separately that ATS is caused by SLC2A10 mutation; one variant row is kept with variant_original_string 'not reported' (the tie to SLC2A10 is through the disease statement, not an explicit statement for this patient). Zygosity not recorded despite the paper calling ATS autosomal recessive (general statement). Main pulmonary artery dilated on day-1 CTA but of normal caliber on repeat CTA (interval change rather than contradiction); both rows emitted with timing. The fig. 4 legend dates the repeat CTA to 8 weeks old while the text places it after re-presentation at 9 weeks. Brain MRA and renal ultrasound 'did not demonstrate additional vascular abnormalities': recorded in the paper's words as excluded; it is not used to exclude 'tortuosity of arteries other than the aorta' since only brain and renal vessels were imaged. Normal growth velocity recorded as excluded 'inappropriate growth velocity' (D-27). Respiratory distress checklist item marked observed from 'respiratory depression at birth' requiring positive-pressure ventilation (perinatal context: emergent cesarean for cord prolapse, chorioamnionitis). Umbilical hernia inferred present from its repair. 'Characteristic facial features' not specified, so down-slanting palpebral fissures is not reported. Age at diagnosis 'at birth' refers to the CTA; age at genetic confirmation not given. Vital status 'alive' from ongoing follow-up at 6 months.",
